><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Hara</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Yonezawa</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Structural requirement of leucine for activation of p70 S6 kinase</article-title><source>FEBS Lett</source><year>1999</year><volume>447</volume><fpage>303</fpage><lpage>306</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10214966</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/S0014-5793(99)00304-X</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B38"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Bodine</surname><given-names>SC</given-names></name><name><surname>Latres</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Baumhueter</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Lai</surname><given-names>VKM</given-names></name><name><surname>Nunez</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><etal></etal></person-group><article-title>Identification of Ubiquitin ligases required for skeletal muscle atrophy</article-title><source>Science</source><year>2001</year><volume>294</volume><fpage>1704</fpage><lpage>1708</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11679633</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1126/science.1065874</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B39"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Latres</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Amini</surname><given-names>AR</given-names></name><name><surname>Amini</surname><given-names>AA</given-names></name><name><surname>Grifiths</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Martin</surname><given-names>FJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Wei</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Lin</surname><given-names>HC</given-names></name><name><surname>Yancopoulos</surname><given-names>GD</given-names></name><name><surname>Glass</surname><given-names>DJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) inversely regulate atrophy-induced genes via the phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase/Akt/mammalian target of rapamycin (PI3k/Akt/mTOR) pathway</article-title><source>J Biol Chem</source><year>2005</year><volume>280</volume><fpage>2737</fpage><lpage>2744</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15550386</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1074/jbc.M407517200</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B40"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Gulve</surname><given-names>EA</given-names></name><name><surname>Dice</surname><given-names>JF</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Regulation of protein synthesis and degradation in L8 myotubes. Effects of serum, insulin and insulin-like growth factors</article-title><source>Biochem J</source><year>1989</year><volume>260</volume><fpage>377</fpage><lpage>387</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2669733</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><sec sec-type="display-objects"><title>Figures and Tables</title><fig position="float" id="F1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p><bold>CPK and protein measurements</bold>. CPK (open diamonds) and protein (filled diamonds) concentration in C2C12 myotubes during 5 days incubation in differentiating media</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-1"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p><bold>Effect of insulin on protein degradation</bold>. Long-lived proteins in C2C12 myotubes were labelled with 1 &#x003bc;Ci <sub>L</sub>[ring -3, 5-<sup>3</sup>H]-tyrosine/ml fusion medium for 48 hours. Myotubes were rinsed twice with 1 &#x000d7; HBSS and incubated in 1 &#x000d7; PC AA media for 2 hours. Myotubes were rinsed and incubated with 10<sup>-9</sup>, 10<sup>-8 </sup>and 10<sup>-5 </sup>M insulin for 8 hours. Samples were collected and percent protein degradation was calculated as described in material and methods. Values are means &#x000b1; SEM from 5 different experiments performed in at least triplicates. * Represents significant difference compared to control (zero insulin).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-2"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p><bold>Amino acids and insulin act additively to regulate total proteolysis in C2C12 myotubes</bold>. Experimental procedures are the same as described under Figure 2. Values are means &#x000b1; SEM for 5 different experiments performed in at least triplicates. * Indicates differences in protein degradation between 0.2 &#x000d7; PC AA (filled bars) &#x00026; 1.0 &#x000d7; PC AA (open bars) treatments. &#x003c8; Represents differences between insulin versus control (without insulin) groups.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-3"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F4"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p><bold>MG132 blocks amino acid mediated total proteolysis</bold>. Experimental procedures are the same as described under Figure 2. Values are means &#x000b1; SEM for 2 different experiments performed in at least triplicates. * Indicates differences in protein degradation between 0.2 &#x000d7; PC AA (filled bars) &#x00026; 1.0 &#x000d7; PC AA (open bars) treatments. &#x003c8; Represents differences between MG132 (50 uM) versus control (without MG132) groups.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-4"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F5"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p><bold>Effect of amino acids and insulin on components of ubiquitin-proteasome pathway</bold>. Experimental procedure is the same as described under Figure 2 except that instead of <sub>L</sub>[ring -3, 5-<sup>3</sup>H]-tyrosine cold tyrosine was used to simulate similar conditions. Values are means &#x000b1; SEM for 3&#x02013;4 experiments performed in triplicates. * Indicates differences in protein degradation between 0.2 &#x000d7; PC AA (filled bars) &#x00026; 1.0 &#x000d7; PC AA (open bars) treatments while &#x003c8; represents differences between insulin versus control (without insulin) groups. Autoradiographic signals were normalised for GAPDH expression. In case of 14-kDa E2, autoradiographic signals for 1.2 kb transcript were normalised. Mean normalised levels of gene expression are represented as arbitrary units.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-5"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F6"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p><bold>Leucine increases the inhibitory effect of insulin on protein degradation in C2C12 myotubes</bold>. Fully differentiated myotubes were pre labelled with <sub>L</sub>[ring -3, 5-<sup>3</sup>H]-tyrosine for 48 hours. Myotubes were rinsed twice with 1 &#x000d7; HBS and chased in 0.2 &#x000d7; PC AA medium for 2 hours followed by incubation with 30 (filled bars), 150 (open bars) and 750 (hatched bars) &#x003bc;M leucine in 0.2 &#x000d7; PC AA medium. Values are means &#x000b1; SEM for two experiments performed in quadruplets. * Represents significant differences among leucine levels whereas &#x003c8; indicates differences relative to control groups.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-6"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F7"><label>Figure 7</label><caption><p><bold>Effect of insulin and 30 (filled bars), 150 (open bars) and 750 (hatched bars) &#x003bc;M leucine on ubiquitin-proteasome pathway</bold>. Experimental procedure is the same as described under Figure 6. Autoradiographic signals normalised for GAPDH were analysed as described under fig 5. * Symbolises significant difference between 30 and 750 &#x003bc;M leucine.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-7"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F8"><label>Figure 8</label><caption><p><bold>Effect of rapamycin on p 70 S6 kinase phosphorylation status in C2C12 myotubes incubated with insulin and 1.0 &#x000d7; PC AA</bold>. Myotubes were serum starved overnight and next morning treated with rapamycin (2.2 &#x000d7; 10<sup>-8 </sup>M) in 1.0 &#x000d7; PC AA for 20 min. Half of the rapamycin treated or non treated cells were then stimulated with insulin (10<sup>-8 </sup>M) for another 20 min. A) immunodetection on western blot, B) respective densitometric analyses. Values are mean &#x000b1; SEM from one experiment performed in duplicates.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-8"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F9"><label>Figure 9</label><caption><p><bold>Effect of rapamycin on total protein degradation</bold>. Myotubes were incubated with 0.2 &#x000d7; PC AA (filled bars) or 1.0 &#x000d7; PC AA (open bars) media (A), or with 30 (filled bars) and 750 (hatched bars) &#x003bc;M leucine in 0.2 &#x000d7; PC AA medium (B). After labelling C2C12 myotubes for 48 hours, cells were incubated with 0.2 &#x000d7; PC AA or 1.0 &#x000d7; PC AA for two hours. Rapamycin (RAP) 2.2 &#x000d7; 10<sup>-8 </sup>M was then added to half of the wells containing 0.2 &#x000d7; PC AA or 1.0 &#x000d7; PC AA and dishes transferred back to incubator. Twenty minutes later insulin 10<sup>-8 </sup>M was added in to half of the rapamycin treated and half of the untreated cells and incubated at 37&#x000b0;C for another 8 hours. Since rapamycin has half-life of about 3&#x02013;4 hours media was supplemented with rapamycin after the first four hours of incubation. Data represent mean &#x000b1; SEM from one or two experiments performed in triplicates. * Indicates differences between two amino acids concentrations, &#x003c8; represents rapamycin treated versus non-rapamycin treated (control) group and # symbolises difference between insulin and rapamycin treated versus insulin treated groups.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-9"/></fig><fig position="float" id="F10"><label>Figure 10</label><caption><p><bold>Effect of rapamycin on the expression of 14-kDa E2 ubiquitin conjugating enzyme in C2C12 myotubes incubated with or without insulin in 1&#x000d7; PC AA</bold>. The same experimental procedure was employed as described under Figure 9A. Values represent mean &#x000b1; SEM from a single experiment performed in triplicates. * Symbolises significant difference relative to control group.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="1471-2199-8-23-10"/></fig><table-wrap position="float" id="T1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Concentration of <sub>L</sub>-amino acids in the 1.0 &#x000d7; physiological concentration (PC) culture media.</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><td align="left"><bold>Amino acids</bold></td><td align="center"><bold>Concentration (mM)</bold></td></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left">Alanine</td><td align="center">0.41</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Arginine</td><td align="center">0.18</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Asparagine</td><td align="center">0.07</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Aspartate</td><td align="center">0.04</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Cysteine</td><td align="center">0.06</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Glutamate</td><td align="center">0.11</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Glutamine</td><td align="center">0.74</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Glycine</td><td align="center">0.28</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Histidine</td><td align="center">0.07</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Isoleucine</td><td align="center">0.09</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Leucine</td><td align="center">0.15</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Lysine</td><td align="center">0.35</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Methionine</td><td align="center">0.05</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Phenylalanine</td><td align="center">0.08</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Proline</td><td align="center">0.33</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Serine</td><td align="center">0.53</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Threonine</td><td align="center">0.25</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Tryptophan</td><td align="center">0.07</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Tyrosine</td><td align="center">0.07</td></tr><tr><td align="left">Valine</td><td align="center">0.19</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></sec></back></article>